{
  "id": "affine_group_schemes.multiplicative_group_scheme",
  "title": "Multiplicative Group Scheme 𝔾_m",
  "kind": "definition",
  "status": "admitted",
  "href": "AlgebraicGroups/AffineGroupSchemes/affine_group_schemes_multiplicative_group_scheme.html",
  "primary_topic": "AlgebraicGroups.AffineGroupSchemes",
  "topics": [
    "AlgebraicGroups.AffineGroupSchemes"
  ],
  "body_html": "<h1>Multiplicative Group Scheme \\(\\mathbb{G}_m\\)</h1>\n<p>Let \\(S\\) be a scheme. The <strong>multiplicative group scheme over \\(S\\)</strong>,\ndenoted \\(\\mathbb{G}_{m,S}\\), is defined by base change:</p>\n<p>\\[\n\\mathbb{G}_{m,S} \\;=\\; S \\,\\times_{\\operatorname{Spec} \\mathbb{Z}}\\, \\operatorname{Spec} \\mathbb{Z}[T, T^{-1}],\n\\]</p>\n<p>where \\(\\mathbb{Z}[T, T^{-1}]\\) is the Laurent polynomial ring (the\nlocalization of \\(\\mathbb{Z}[T]\\) at the multiplicative set generated by\n\\(T\\)). Its coordinate Hopf algebra is \\(\\mathbb{Z}[T, T^{-1}]\\) with the\n<strong>multiplicative Hopf structure</strong>:</p>\n<p>\\[\n\\Delta(T) = T \\otimes T, \\qquad\n\\varepsilon(T) = 1, \\qquad\nS(T) = T^{-1}.\n\\]</p>\n<p>The structure morphism \\(\\mathbb{G}_{m,S} \\to S\\) is affine and locally\nof finite type. Hence \\(\\mathbb{G}_{m,S}\\) is an\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"affine_group_schemes.algebraic_group_definition\" href=\"../../affine_group_schemes/affine_group_schemes_algebraic_group_definition.html\">algebraic group</a>\nover \\(S\\); it is smooth of relative dimension one and is commutative.\nIts functor of points is identified by\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"affine_group_schemes.multiplicative_group_scheme_points\" href=\"../../affine_group_schemes/affine_group_schemes_multiplicative_group_scheme_points.html\">the multiplicative points formula</a>.</p>\n<p>The group scheme \\(\\mathbb{G}_m\\) is the rank-one case of the family of\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"linear_algebraic_groups.basic_examples\" href=\"../../linear_algebraic_groups/linear_algebraic_groups_basic_examples.html\">split tori</a> \\(T = \\mathbb{G}_m^n\\);\nits character lattice is \\(X^*(\\mathbb{G}_m) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}\\), generated\nby the identity character \\(T\\).</p>",
  "proof_html": null,
